<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        A budget says what you mean to spend in each category over a year. Set it on the <a href="budget">Budget</a> page, starting
        from LoonieBooks' suggestions, then follow how you're doing on the dashboard's <strong>Budget vs spending</strong> chart.
    </p>

    <h3>Setting a budget</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Pick the year at the top right. Each year has its own budget, so you can compare one year's with the next.</li>
        <li>Type an amount in a category's <strong>budget</strong> box; it's saved when you leave the box. Empty it (or enter 0) to
            remove the budget.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Per month</span> and <span class="help-ui">Per year</span> switch every amount on the page, the
            boxes included. A budget is always for the whole year: type 500 per month and it's saved as 6,000 for the year. A month's
            share is a twelfth of it, so per month, lumpy costs like insurance or property tax look over in the month they're paid
            and under the rest; per year, they even out.</li>
        <li><strong>Yearly</strong>: tick it for costs that come a few times a year, like tuition, tax instalments or an annual
            insurance premium. A yearly budget has no monthly share: its amounts stay a year's (marked <em>/yr</em>) even per month,
            the totals per month add it up apart as <strong>Yearly budgets</strong>, and the dashboard's chart leaves it out of its
            months and counts it under Years. LoonieBooks ticks it in advance for a category whose spending came in a third of the
            months or fewer (4 of 12, say); untick it to keep a monthly share.</li>
        <li>The arrow beside a category opens its details (Food &amp; Dining › Groceries, Restaurants…). A category's own budget
            counts all its spending, details included; you can also set budgets on details as limits inside it. A category with no
            budget of its own can be budgeted detail by detail instead, and its total is then the details' added up.</li>
        <li>Small categories, with under 1% of your spending (none at all included) and no budget this year or last, are
            gathered into one <strong>Other</strong> row at the bottom, so the budget isn't cluttered with them. Open it to see
            them and budget one if you like; a category you give a budget moves up into the list.</li>
        <li>Income, transfers between your accounts and uncategorized money don't have budgets. Uncategorized spending counts against
            no budget until it's categorized, so the page says how much there is, with a link to it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Suggestions</h3>
    <p>
        Each category's suggestion comes from last year's spending in it. For a monthly budget it's the average month, rounded up
        to a tidy amount (to the next 5 under 50, 10 under 250, 25 under 1,000, then 50 and 100), so CA$716.67 a month becomes
        CA$725. For a yearly budget it's the year, rounded up the same way (to the next 50 under 1,000, 100 under 10,000, then 500
        and 1,000). Click a suggestion to use it, or <span class="help-ui">Use suggestions</span> to fill in every category
        without a budget at once, apart from those under Other.
        <span class="help-ui">Copy … budget</span> does the same with last year's budget, when it had one. Both keep each line
        yearly or monthly as its <strong>Yearly</strong> box shows.
    </p>
    <p>
        Without a whole last year to go by (you started importing this spring, say), suggestions come from the latest complete
        months you have, up to 12, counted as a year: five months of spending times 12 ÷ 5. A first month that starts after its first
        week is left out, as only part of it was imported. Seasonal costs may be missing from a short stretch, so look over the
        suggestions before using them.
    </p>

    <h3>What counts</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Spending is counted the way the dashboard counts it: refunds reduce spending in their category, and transfers between your
            accounts and credit card payments aren't spending.</li>
        <li>Everything is in Canadian dollars. Spending in an account kept in another currency is converted at the Bank of Canada's
            average rate for its month (the latest rate for the month under way), so last year's figures stay put when the dollar
            moves. LoonieBooks fetches those rates itself; only the currency is sent.</li>
        <li><strong>Spent so far</strong> is this year's spending in budgeted categories, with the share of the budget it is and the
            share of the year that's gone. The bar beside each category fills with its spending (red past the budget), and the
            short line on it is where spending would be by today at an even pace.</li>
        <li>Per month, the spending column is the average month so far this year.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>On the dashboard</h3>
    <p>
        Switch the dashboard's chart with <span class="help-ui">‹</span> or <span class="help-ui">›</span> to
        <strong>Budget vs spending by month</strong>, each month against its share of the budget (yearly budgets left out; a month's
        tooltip lists what was spent against them), or to <strong>Budget status</strong>, each category against its budget:
        <span class="help-ui">Month</span> for a month against the monthly budgets, <span class="help-ui">Year</span> for the year so
        far against the whole budgets, yearly ones included, with the even pace for today. Budgets are the household's, so the
        charts cover every account and tag even when the dashboard is narrowed to some. See <a href="help?a=dashboard">Dashboard</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="help-note">
        Budgets are your own plan for your spending, kept with your ledger and in its backups. LoonieBooks doesn't judge what a
        budget should be.
    </p>
</div>
